      "job-hunt-automation": {
        slug: "job-hunt-automation",
        title: "In simple terms, how does job-hunt automation work?",
        category: "ARCHITECTURE & PIPELINES",
        date: "September 2026",
        readTime: "3 min read",
        content: `
          <p class="blog-p lead-p">I wanted to reduce the time I spent looking for and applying for jobs, so I automated the majority of that process using AI, specifically AI agents.</p>
          <p class="blog-p">In simple terms, an AI agent pairs an LLM (like Claude, ChatGPT, or Gemini) which acts as the 'brain', with a framework of files and rules that dictate how that 'brain' thinks, operates, and uses connected tools.</p>
          <p class="blog-p">I designed this workflow from scratch, then built a team of specialised agents to help me write the code and convert the workflow into automated scripts. The agents do not run this workflow; the workflow runs on a schedule (crons). The agents' roles are now limited to:</p>
          <ul class="blog-list">
            <li><strong>Fixes and self-healing:</strong> Each day an automatic script runs to check for breaking updates in the software the workflow uses. If any are found, an agent automatically updates the workflow to keep it working.</li>
            <li><strong>CV tailoring:</strong> One agent is used as part of the workflow to create tailored CVs for each role I want to apply for.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="blog-subheading">What are the steps in the workflow?</h3>
          <ol class="blog-list blog-steps-list">
            <li><strong>Scrapes the internet:</strong> Finds newly listed jobs that match what I am looking for across major boards.</li>
            <li><strong>Scores each job:</strong> Evaluates how well it matches my criteria, filtering out weak matches and recruitment spam so I do not waste time on irrelevant listings.</li>
            <li><strong>Researches company culture (vibe check):</strong> Instead of looking only at qualifications, it checks company ethics, controversies, employee sentiment, and workplace culture. The system excludes companies based on hard lines or adjusts priority scores accordingly.</li>
            <li><strong>Populates Mission Control:</strong> Top-ranking roles flow into a custom dashboard running locally on my computer. I can view job descriptions and vibe scores all in one place, then click Yes or No to decide whether to proceed.</li>
            <li><strong>Tailors a CV on approval:</strong> The system instructs Cordy to craft a role-specific CV. Cordy checks the application URL to adapt formatting for applicant tracking systems (ATS), strictly anchored to my master CV with zero invented facts.</li>
            <li><strong>Human-in-the-loop review and PDF generation:</strong> I review the markdown CV and make edits if needed. Cordy notes any edits to improve future outputs. With one click, an automated script compiles the markdown into a styled PDF.</li>
            <li><strong>Manual submission:</strong> I review and submit the application myself with the tailored CV, saving 83% of the time while protecting application quality.</li>
          </ol>

          <h3 class="blog-subheading">Self-healing and continuous learning</h3>
          <p class="blog-p">The workflow also maintains itself. An automated script runs daily to verify dependencies. If an API or tool breaks, Willow (the builder agent) is automatically called in to patch the code using real live context.</p>
          <p class="blog-p">Cordy also learns continuously. When I provide feedback on why a role was rejected or why a CV was phrased awkwardly, Cordy saves that feedback into memory, making future runs sharper.</p>
        `
      },
      "what-is-an-ai-agent": {
        slug: "what-is-an-ai-agent",
        title: "What is an AI agent?",
        category: "AGENTIC CONCEPTS",
        date: "September 2026",
        readTime: "2 min read",
        content: `
          <p class="blog-p lead-p">Most people are familiar with AI through chatbots like ChatGPT, Claude, or Gemini. A standard chatbot only responds when prompted: you ask a question, it replies with text, and then it stops. You still have to do all the manual follow-up work: copying the output, pasting it into other software, running checks, and deciding the next action.</p>
          <p class="blog-p">An AI agent goes much further. Instead of just answering questions, an agent is given an overall goal and the tools needed to complete it autonomously.</p>
          
          <h3 class="blog-subheading">The Three Core Pillars</h3>
          <ul class="blog-list">
            <li><strong>The Brain:</strong> A Large Language Model that provides reasoning, understands context, and decides what actions to take.</li>
            <li><strong>The Rulebook and Memory:</strong> Clear rules, preferences, and guidelines that dictate how the agent should think, what standards it must follow, and what boundaries it cannot cross.</li>
            <li><strong>The Tools:</strong> Connected software, scripts, web browsers, and file access that allow the agent to interact with the computer: searching the internet, reading documents, generating files, or running code.</li>
          </ul>

          <h3 class="blog-subheading">Why do people use them?</h3>
          <p class="blog-p">People build and use AI agents to automate multi-step digital workflows that previously required hours of repetitive manual effort:</p>
          <ul class="blog-list">
            <li><strong>Multi-step execution:</strong> Instead of doing one task at a time, an agent can break a complex objective into smaller steps, evaluate intermediate results, and self-correct if something goes wrong.</li>
            <li><strong>Connecting separate software:</strong> Agents link different tools together (such as web scrapers, local databases, and messaging systems) without needing a person to act as the manual bridge between them.</li>
            <li><strong>Consistent standards:</strong> An agent applies the same rigorous quality checks and rules every single time, avoiding human fatigue during monotonous tasks.</li>
          </ul>
          <p class="blog-p">In this project, agents helped convert the pipeline design into automated Python scripts during the build phase. In daily operation, their runtime role is kept deliberate: Cordy tailors CVs against verified ground-truth data and learns from feedback, while Willow handles automated self-healing if daily dependency checks detect broken tools. Scheduled crons and deterministic scripts handle the rest with zero AI overhead.</p>
        `
      },
